      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "James Emery from Douglasville, United States",
      "description": "The Raven UAV \"has about 50 minutes of flight time on a battery. The kit comes with spare batteries and a charger so [the crew] can land it, pop in a spare and get it back in the air. It can go as fast as 90 [k/h], but usually cruises between 40 and 50. It can go as far as 15 kilometers from its controller on the ground, and usually flies a pre-programmed route, using GPS for navigation.\nThree different cameras can attach to the plane [electrical optical, infrared (nose), and a side-mounted IR]. The camera does not have zoom capability and is unable to lock on a target, but provides enough resolution to show someone carrying a weapon.\nRavens are easily hand-launched by turning on the motor and throwing it in the air. It lands by coming back to ground at a point designated by GPS location. . . On average, a Raven aircraft can survive about 200 landings before it breaks something.\"\n\nfrom Army Aviation Museum placard",
      "dateCreated": "2007-09-29 14:33",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "united-states-army-aviation-museum-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "dje4",
